.TH PICOFETCH 1 "November 15, 2026"
.SH NAME
picofetch \- show system and hardware readings beside a logo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B picofetch
[
.B \-lnp
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Picofetch
prints one screen of readings about the running system: the operating
system and kernel build, the board, uptime, the processor and bus clocks,
the second core, memory, swap, each mounted file system, the drives, the
flash image and its checksum, the flash chip's identity, the
execute-in-place cache, the memory
protection unit, the display, the battery and backlights of a PicoCalc,
its audio device, the temperature and supply voltage, the load averages, the fixed kernel
tables, the kernel stack, the storage counters, the USB console and the
cause of the last reset.
.PP
Every value is read from the kernel through
.IR sysctl (3)
and
.IR getfsstat (2),
from the terminal or from the environment.
A reading the running kernel does not serve prints
.B "not in this kernel"
and one whose read fails prints
.BR "read failed" .
.I Picofetch
changes nothing in the kernel and needs no privilege.
.PP
On the PicoCalc console the logo and the level bars are drawn from the
console's glyph bank in its sixteen colors: a bar resolves a sixth of a
character cell.
The glyphs stay in the bank after
.I picofetch
exits, so the report stays on the screen; the console's reset sequence
restores the bank.
On any other terminal the same layout is drawn in ASCII with ANSI colors.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B \-l
Print no logo.
A terminal narrower than 46 columns gets none either.
.TP
.B \-n
Write no color.
The environment variable
.B NO_COLOR
has the same effect.
.TP
.B \-p
Plain output: ASCII and no escape sequence.
This is the default when the output is not a terminal.
.PP
The chip line shows the JEDEC bytes and the 64-bit unique ID of the QSPI
flash (\fBmachdep.flash_jedec\fP, \fBmachdep.flash_unique_hi\fP and \fBmachdep.flash_unique_lo\fP), or
.B "identity unread"
when the chip returned no plausible answer at boot.
The audio line shows the sample rate, channels and bits of
.I /dev/audio
with whether a process has it open and how many samples are queued
(\fBmachdep.pcm\fP); kernels without the device print
.BR "not in this kernel" .
The processor line adds the peripheral clock only when it differs from the
core clock, and the battery line carries the panel and key backlight levels,
so that the report stays within 30 rows of the 32-row console.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
picotop(1), ps(1), sysctl(8), sysctl(3)
